			"10": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": false
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "EXAMPLE 2 : NEAREST COLOR\n\nIn this first example we take two colors (A and B) and join them together in a collection.\nNext, we take a search color and the nearest node to find which color is nearest to the search color.\nThis gives us the index, this index is used with the Read node to read the correct color from the original collection.\n"
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 562.7664794921875,
					"width": 1490.4949951171875,
					"x": -725.886962890625,
					"y": 396.56378173828125
				},
				"class": {
					"id": "77697265-3C1D-467D-A722-0FC566538374",
					"version": 1
				},
				"clock": "video",
				"color": "fff26eb5",
				"constants": {},
				"hidden": [
					"text",
					"alignment",
					"font-size",
					"fill"
				],
				"name": "Comment",
				"thumbnail_visible": true
			},
			"175": {
				"attributes": {
					"alignment": {
						"type": "integer",
						"value": 0
					},
					"fill": {
						"type": "bool",
						"value": true
					},
					"font-size": {
						"type": "float",
						"value": 19
					},
					"text": {
						"type": "string",
						"value": "NEAREST\n\nThe Nearest node takes a collection of values and outputs the index of the value that is nearest to the search value. \n\nComputing compound float types like Float2, Float3 and Float4 allows you to weigh your values - telling Wire which element of the type is more important for the calculation.\n\nThis node and it's opposite node \"Furthest\" are great tools for making behaviors.\nYou can calculate which position is closest to the position of an instance and make changes based on that outcome. "
					}
				},
				"bounds": {
					"height": 195.59197998046875,
					"width": 835.638427734375,
					"x": -727.264892578125,
